// src/user/aml/aml_thermal.pdx — R31.M2-004 (#1097)
//
// The AML reader for ACPI 6.5 §11.4's four ThermalZone methods --
// _TMP (current temperature), _CRT (critical trip), _HOT (hot trip),
// _PSV (passive trip). Each yields an integer in DECI-KELVIN. This
// module is the door through which those integers reach the userspace
// side of the thermal subsystem, and eventually -- via a supervisor-
// side syscall path that does not yet exist -- KIND_THERMAL_ZONE in
// the kernel.
//
// =====================================================================
// PLACEMENT -- CONSTITUTIONAL
// =====================================================================
// Userspace only. See design/acpi/no-aml-in-kernel.md. This file
// evaluates firmware bytecode (indirectly, through aml_eval_method) and
// must never link into src/kernel/**; tools/lint-no-kernel-aml.sh
// refuses aml* symbols there and would fail the build if a well-meaning
// refactor moved the reader into ring 0.
//
// =====================================================================
// WHY THIS IS A THIN MODULE OVER aml_eval AND NOT A SECOND EVALUATOR
// =====================================================================
// A dedicated "static-integer subset" evaluator was considered and
// refused: two evaluators means two termination guarantees, two frame
// pools, two fuel counters, and two answers for the same table when
// they drift. Every ACPI 6.5 §11.4 method is a plain Method returning
// an Integer -- and Method invocation, integer-return handling, fuel
// accounting and depth bounding are exactly what aml_eval delivers.
// So this module does two things and no more:
//
//   (1) Resolve the child NameSeg exactly inside the ThermalZone's own
//       scope. Exactly, not by search, for the same reason
//       aml_ec_query_pump uses aml_eval_find_in_scope rather than
//       aml_eval_resolve: a search would find some ancestor's _CRT and
//       apply it to a zone that never declared one.
//   (2) Recognise the two legal shapes -- Method(_XXX,0){Return(N)} or
//       Name(_XXX,N) -- and pick the right primitive for each.
//
// It does NOT construct a KIND_THERMAL_ZONE mint payload, and it does
// NOT drive THERMAL_OP_ASSESS. Those are supervisor concerns: the
// syscall wall through which a userspace reader reaches the kernel-
// side kind is R31.M2-005+ work and cannot happen from a module whose
// effect row lists only {mem}. Writing them here would make this
// module both the AML reader and the acpi_supervisor's thermal shim,
// which is the two-things-in-one shape ec_query_pump exists NOT to be.
//
// =====================================================================
// SHAPES AND THEIR REFUSALS
// =====================================================================
// ACPI 6.5 §11.4 permits both a Name(_CRT, 3730) and a Method(_CRT,0)
// { Return(3730) } for the trip-point methods -- and firmware writers
// pick whichever is convenient. _TMP is almost always a Method (it
// reads a live sensor); _CRT/_HOT/_PSV are more often Names, but a
// system whose critical threshold varies with thermal profile (a Fn+F
// tunable, a battery/AC transition) writes a Method that consults an
// EC byte and returns it. Refusing one shape would refuse real
// firmware. So this reader accepts both:
//
//   AML_NODE_NAME (5) with data kind 1 (Integer) -> aml_eval_read_named
//   AML_NODE_METHOD (4) with argcount 0          -> aml_eval_method
//
// Anything else is a table bug and is surfaced as AML_THERMAL_ERR
// rather than as a zero, because the whole posture of the AML
// subsystem is that a WRONG VALUE IS WORSE THAN NO VALUE. A Method
// with non-zero arity for a _TMP name is the single most likely form
// of that bug: a resolver that ignored the seg's semantics could match
// any Method whose final NameSeg happened to spell _TMP -- a vendor's
// diagnostic hook, for instance -- and would evaluate it with garbage
// arguments. The arity check is what makes the NameSeg mean the method
// ACPI defined and not a same-named thing beside it.
//
// =====================================================================
// UNITS
// =====================================================================
// EVERY VALUE THIS READER RETURNS IS IN DECI-KELVIN, per ACPI 6.5
// §11.4. No conversion here, ever. The kernel-side kind
// (core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx §0) is emphatic about this and for
// good reason -- a conversion in the reader would be the one place a
// °C-vs-K sign error is invisible until temperatures start looking
// impossibly hot or impossibly cold. The one comment in this module
// mentioning Celsius does so to say that this module does not.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE THREE POSSIBLE OUTCOMES OF A READ
// =====================================================================
// A single integer value cannot encode "found and read", "not present"
// and "evaluator faulted" as one dimension. They are three different
// outcomes with three different caller responses:
//
//   AML_THERMAL_OK       -- returned as a bare 0..u32max value. This
//                           is the value; store it, ratchet the row.
//   AML_THERMAL_ABSENT   -- the ThermalZone did not declare this name.
//                           Legal for _HOT and _PSV per §11.4 (both
//                           optional trips); ILLEGAL for _CRT (a zone
//                           without _CRT cannot mint per §1 of
//                           core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx) and
//                           dubious for _TMP (a zone that reports no
//                           temperature has nothing for a policy
//                           server to act on). The reader does not
//                           make the policy call -- it reports the
//                           absence honestly and the caller decides
//                           whether to refuse the whole mint.
//   AML_THERMAL_ERR      -- evaluator latched. The caller must
//                           aml_eval_reset before the next attempt and
//                           should assume the zone is unreadable this
//                           boot until proven otherwise.
//
// Both sentinels are chosen at the top of the u64 range, so they
// cannot collide with a plausible deci-Kelvin value: 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE
// as a temperature is ~4.6 * 10^17 K, and there is no such thing. This
// is the same disambiguation shape AML_EC_XACT_FAIL uses: one call
// yields both the outcome and the value, and a caller that forgets to
// check the sentinel cannot mistake a refusal for data.
//
// =====================================================================
// DEPENDENCIES
// =====================================================================
// This module reads through the accessor API only -- it touches no
// arena directly and holds no state of its own. tools/verify-aml-
// parser.sh has one MODULES entry to add and no new check_confined
// call, so adding aml_thermal.o strengthens every existing storage
// confinement by proving the reader reaches none of it.
//
// The four aml_eval calls this file makes -- aml_eval_find_in_scope,
// aml_eval_method, aml_eval_read_named, aml_eval_err (and the one
// aml_node_kind / aml_method_argcount pair) -- are exactly what a
// static-integer evaluator would need. That the reader can be written
// as a hundred lines of dispatch over the existing surface is the
// evidence that #1054-#1060's evaluator was factored the right way.

module AmlThermal = structure {

  // =====================================================================
  // The four §11.4 NameSegs, encoded as little-endian u32 to match
  // aml_lex_nameseg's packing. Written as the exact byte values a
  // memory dump would show, so a reader can eyeball them against the
  // spec without re-deriving the endian rule for each character.
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'T' = 0x54, 'M' = 0x4D, 'P' = 0x50 -> 0x504D545F
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'C' = 0x43, 'R' = 0x52, 'T' = 0x54 -> 0x5452435F
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'H' = 0x48, 'O' = 0x4F, 'T' = 0x54 -> 0x544F485F
  //   '_' = 0x5F, 'P' = 0x50, 'S' = 0x53, 'V' = 0x56 -> 0x5653505F
  // =====================================================================

  pub let AML_THERMAL_SEG_TMP : u64 = 0x504D545F
  pub let AML_THERMAL_SEG_CRT : u64 = 0x5452435F
  pub let AML_THERMAL_SEG_HOT : u64 = 0x544F485F
  pub let AML_THERMAL_SEG_PSV : u64 = 0x5653505F

  // =====================================================================
  // Outcome sentinels. Both live outside the plausible integer range so
  // they cannot collide with a legitimate reading, and they occupy the
  // top two u64 values so the failure predicate is a single unsigned
  // compare (`value >= AML_THERMAL_ABSENT`) rather than two.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let AML_THERMAL_ABSENT : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE
  pub let AML_THERMAL_ERR    : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // The ThermalZone kind, mirrored from aml_arena.pdx's node-kind
  // enumeration. Named here rather than imported so a future kind
  // renumbering fails to compile at exactly one place per module that
  // depends on the value, which is what forces the migration to be
  // deliberate everywhere.
  pub let AML_THERMAL_NODE_KIND : u64 = 9

  // =====================================================================
  // aml_thermal_read_child(zone_node, seg) -> deci_kelvin
  //                                         | AML_THERMAL_ABSENT
  //                                         | AML_THERMAL_ERR
  //
  // THE ONE FUNCTION THAT KNOWS BOTH SHAPES. The four public wrappers
  // below are `mov rsi, <seg>; jmp <this>` in spirit -- the shape
  // dispatch and every error taxonomy sits here.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_thermal_read_child : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (zone_node: u64) (seg: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): resolve NameSeg rsi as an exact child of ThermalZone node rdi and read its integer value. FOUR OUTCOMES, THREE PATHS. (a) rdi == 0 or its arena kind != AML_THERMAL_NODE_KIND (9) -> AML_THERMAL_ERR, because the caller has handed us a scope with no thermal semantics and returning ABSENT would let a subsequent walker treat an unrelated Method's zero as 'this zone has no critical trip'. (b) aml_eval_find_in_scope answers 0 without latching -> AML_THERMAL_ABSENT, the honest report of an optional trip that firmware chose not to declare. (c) A resolved AML_NODE_METHOD (4) with argcount 0 -> aml_eval_method; a resolved AML_NODE_NAME (5) (delegated to aml_eval_read_named, which enforces data-kind 1 = Integer) -> the value. Any other node kind, or a Method with non-zero arity, is AML_THERMAL_ERR: the resolver matched a same-named object that is not one of ACPI 6.5 §11.4's four methods, and evaluating it would produce a wrong answer for the wrong reason. THE aml_eval_err POST-CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING: aml_eval_method's return of 0 is ambiguous between 'the method returned zero' and 'evaluation faulted', so a caller that trusted the raw return would let a fault masquerade as an implausibly cold trip point -- which core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx §3 records as one of the two ways a bad reading is dangerous. THE aml_eval_retval_is_obj POST-CHECK IS THE COUNTERPART: a Method that returned a Buffer or Package is a table shape §11.4 does not define, and while aml_eval_method unwraps Integers to their bare value it hands back an object index (with the tag set) for anything else; the tag says that number is an arena index, not a temperature. Locals: r12=zone_node, r13=seg, r14=resolved node / retval scratch, r15=node kind. Labels prefixed aml_thc_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // zone_node
        mov r13, rsi;                    // seg

        // (a) The scope must be a ThermalZone, and it must be a real
        // node. A zero node index is the arena's sentinel and would
        // make find_in_scope compute the absolute path of "no scope",
        // which is the root -- and a search-from-root of a bare
        // NameSeg is exactly the failure mode aml_eval_find_in_scope
        // exists NOT to have.
        cmp r12, 0;
        je aml_thc_err;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 9;                      // AML_THERMAL_NODE_KIND
        jne aml_thc_err;

        // Resolve exactly in the zone's own scope. On a miss,
        // find_in_scope returns 0 WITHOUT LATCHING -- an absent
        // optional trip is not an error.
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        call aml_eval_find_in_scope;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je aml_thc_maybe_absent;
        mov r14, rax;                    // resolved node

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_node_kind;
        mov r15, rax;

        cmp r15, 4;                      // AML_NODE_METHOD
        je aml_thc_method;
        cmp r15, 5;                      // AML_NODE_NAME
        je aml_thc_name;

        // Any other kind (Alias, Field element, Device, etc.) is a
        // shape §11.4 does not define. A silent zero here would look
        // exactly like a plausible reading; an error latch tells the
        // caller "the resolver found something with this NameSeg but
        // it is not what ACPI meant".
        jmp aml_thc_err;

      aml_thc_method:
        // (b) A Method that takes arguments is not this method. The
        // resolver could not have known -- Method arity is namespace-
        // wide identity, but the NameSeg alone is not -- so the arity
        // check is what turns a matched NameSeg into a matched method.
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_method_argcount;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_thc_err;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_eval_method;
        mov r14, rax;                    // provisional value

        // The method returned zero AND an error latched -- that is not
        // "the method returned zero", it is "evaluation faulted". The
        // check happens BEFORE the object-tag check because a fault
        // clears no tag and an implementation that inverted the order
        // would report ERR on a real fault via the retval path with
        // the wrong code.
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_thc_err;

        // A non-Integer return has the retval tag set. §11.4 permits
        // no such return; refusing it here surfaces the table bug at
        // its origin rather than letting an arena index propagate to a
        // trip-point store as a plausible small number.
        call aml_eval_retval_is_obj;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_thc_err;

        mov rax, r14;
        jmp aml_thc_out;

      aml_thc_name:
        // aml_eval_read_named refuses anything other than an Integer-
        // valued NAME with AML_ERR_NOT_EVALUABLE (35); a String or
        // Buffer NAME therefore lands here as an ERR and not as a
        // zero. The err post-check is what turns the read's 0-return
        // into either "the name held zero" (OK) or "the read
        // refused" (ERR).
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_eval_read_named;
        mov r14, rax;
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_thc_err;
        mov rax, r14;
        jmp aml_thc_out;

      aml_thc_maybe_absent:
        // find_in_scope is silent on a genuine miss. If SOMETHING
        // latched during the resolution (a malformed path, a
        // NAME_TOO_DEEP), propagate that as ERR rather than papering
        // over it as ABSENT -- one caller confusing the two would try
        // to mint a zone with an "optional" _CRT that was actually a
        // resolver fault. If nothing latched, this is honestly an
        // absent optional trip, which is legal per §11.4.
        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne aml_thc_err;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE;     // AML_THERMAL_ABSENT
        jmp aml_thc_out;

      aml_thc_err:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // AML_THERMAL_ERR

      aml_thc_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // =====================================================================
  // The four §11.4 wrappers. Each is a call to aml_thermal_read_child
  // with the seg baked in -- a wrapper rather than a macro so the
  // symbol table names the reading (a caller that stack-traces through
  // a fault sees `aml_thermal_read_tmp`, not a raw seg constant) and so
  // the pinning of "which four NameSegs this module reads" is a
  // property of the API rather than of comment discipline.
  //
  // No prologue push: the wrappers are pure argument setup + tail-call
  // shape, one push for rsi handoff. `call` rather than `jmp` because
  // paideia-as does not lower tail calls and a call-return keeps the
  // stack pattern the debugger's `bt` command expects on this platform.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_thermal_read_tmp : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (zone_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): read the ThermalZone's _TMP method into a deci-Kelvin value. In practice _TMP is a Method that touches the embedded controller for a live sample; a Name(_TMP, const) would be a debug fixture. Both are accepted by the shared path so no fixture author has to shape a table around a reader that only takes one form. Locals: rdi = zone_node, unmodified. Labels prefixed aml_tt_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x504D545F;             // AML_THERMAL_SEG_TMP
        call aml_thermal_read_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_thermal_read_crt : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (zone_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): read the ThermalZone's _CRT critical trip. WHEN THIS RETURNS AML_THERMAL_ABSENT THE CALLER MUST NOT MINT A KIND_THERMAL_ZONE FOR THIS ZONE. core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx §1 refuses a zone without a plausible _CRT (THERMAL_MINT_BAD_CRT), and a policy server that installed a zone whose _CRT it substituted for a default would be silently protecting the machine at a threshold ACPI never sanctioned. Labels prefixed aml_tc_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x5452435F;             // AML_THERMAL_SEG_CRT
        call aml_thermal_read_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_thermal_read_hot : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (zone_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): read the ThermalZone's _HOT hibernate trip. §11.4 makes _HOT optional, and this kernel deliberately does not implement hibernation (core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx §1: '_HOT is DELIBERATELY ABSENT rather than stored and ignored'). Reading it here anyway is honest exposure: the reader is generic; whether to ACT on a returned value is a policy decision that lives in the supervisor and not in the AML layer. A supervisor that ignores this value has a defensible position; a reader that suppressed the read would hide a datum a later hibernation path could not recover. Labels prefixed aml_th_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x544F485F;             // AML_THERMAL_SEG_HOT
        call aml_thermal_read_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_thermal_read_psv : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (zone_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): read the ThermalZone's _PSV passive-cooling trip. §11.4 makes _PSV optional and firmware routinely omits it on machines that defer passive cooling to hardware HWP -- which is the T14 G4 posture per design/roadmap/next-wave-osarch.md §2.3. AML_THERMAL_ABSENT is therefore a common and legitimate answer for this reader on modern silicon; core/cap/kind_thermal_zone.pdx encodes an absent _PSV as psv_dk = 0 in the row and treats that as 'no passive trip requested'. Labels prefixed aml_tp_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 0x5653505F;             // AML_THERMAL_SEG_PSV
        call aml_thermal_read_child;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // =====================================================================
  // aml_thermal_is_ok(value) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // The predicate a caller needs after every read: value is a real
  // deci-Kelvin quantity, not a sentinel. One unsigned compare because
  // the sentinels are the top two u64 values -- see the AML_THERMAL_*
  // constant block.
  // =====================================================================

  pub let aml_thermal_is_ok : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-004 (#1097): 1 iff value is a plausible reading and not one of the two failure sentinels. A single `jae` against AML_THERMAL_ABSENT (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) covers both AML_THERMAL_ABSENT and AML_THERMAL_ERR because the two sentinels are the top two u64 values -- and adding a third sentinel later needs a wider window, not a second branch, which pins the encoding rather than the code. Provided as a function rather than a macro so the predicate is auditable at one address; a caller that hand-inlined the compare could get the direction wrong and treat every reading as a refusal. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed aml_tio_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE;     // AML_THERMAL_ABSENT
        cmp rdi, r10;
        jae aml_tio_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      aml_tio_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame
}
